# How much does monopoly cost society?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "How big is the deadweight triangle? Why did Tullock think the real cost is the fight to become the monopolist?"
next: {"title":"Is monopoly always bad?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/34-is-monopoly-always-bad.cast"}

## A tenth of a percent
domain: {"x":[0,240],"y":[0,20]}
sources:
    - id: harberger
      title: Monopoly and Resource Allocation
      authors: Harberger, A. C.
      year: 1954
      finding: 'Using profit rates of 73 US manufacturing industries in 1924–28, the welfare loss from monopoly misallocation came to roughly a tenth of one percent of national income (his own wording: no more than a thirteenth of a percent).'
    - id: cm
      title: The Social Costs of Monopoly Power
      authors: Cowling, K. & Mueller, D. C.
      year: 1978
      finding: Restates Harberger (1954) as 0.1% of GNP; with firm-level data and rent-seeking costs included, estimates 4–13% of gross corporate product for 734 large US firms, 1963–66.
      doi: 10.2307/2231975

    text harb_p "Arnold Harberger, 1954" x 500 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    scale guess cites harberger y 380 label "Cost of monopoly, US 1920s" unit % min 0 max 5 value 0.1 hidden true
    text big "0.1% of income" #b5482e cites harberger x 500 y 560 font_size 64 hidden true
    axes axes x_label "Tickets a day (Q)" y_label "Price ($)" cross corner ticks.x [80,160] ticks.y [4,12] hidden true
    icon ferry at.data [205,17] size 90 set twemoji icon_look picture of ferry hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "20 - 0.1*x" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_D "D" #b5482e at.data [204,1.2] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "4" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_MC "MC" #2f6b8f at.data [209,4] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pc #3d3833 at.on D at.x 160 hidden true
    text label_pc "Competitive: 160 at $4" at.data [196,5.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot pm #3d3833 at.on D at.x 80 hidden true
    text label_pm "Monopoly: 80 at $12" at.data [113,13.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    curve gh #8f887c expr "12" x_from 0 x_to 80 dash true hidden true
    curve gv #8f887c x_expr "80" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 12 dash true hidden true
    region dwl x_from 80 x_to 160 between ["D","MC"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text n_dwl "DWL $320" #b5482e at.data [165,8.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    region rect x_from 0 x_to 80 between ["gh","MC"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text n_tr "transfer?" at.data [40,8] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "A tenth of a percent"

How much does monopoly cost society? In 1954 a you (@draw harb_p@)ng economist, Arnold Harberger, set out to put a number on it.

B: He took American manufacturing in the late 1920s, seventy-three indust (@draw guess@)ries, and added up what their market power cost the country in a year.
    erase harb_p

    ask "What share of national income did monopoly cost, by his count? Click the line." intro "Your guess first." on guess store g default "2.5" voice b
        right: Well judged: you said {g}, he found about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; he found about {g.true}.

About a tenth of on (@draw big@)e percent of national income. Small change.

    erase guess

B: Then why does every rich country keep a competition authority? Either monopoly is nearly harmless, or he measured the wrong thing.
    highlight big effect underline

    erase big

To see what he measured, take one monopoly in Harbourtown: the only ferry to the mainland, with illustrative numbers.
    draw axes D label_D ferry

Carrying one mor (@draw MC label_MC@)e passenger costs the ferry four dollars. If ferries competed, tickets would sell at that cost, and a hun (@draw pc label_pc@)dred and sixty people a day would cross.

B: But a lone ferry does what any monopolist does. I (@draw pm label_pm gh gv@)t sells fewer tickets, eighty a day, at twelve dollars each.

Eighty people who would pay more than the four dollars their crossing costs, but not twelve, now stay at home.
    point at.data [120,8] gesture underline

B: Those lost trips are the deadweight  (@draw dwl n_dwl@)triangle: here, three hundred and twenty dollars a day of value that simply vanishes.

    pause 0.3

That triangle is all Harberger counted, industry by industry. And triangles are small, because they only count the trades that never happen.
    highlight dwl effect pulse

B: And the rectangle  (@draw rect@)above the cost line? Every passenger who still crosses pays eight dollars more than the crossing costs.

In Harberger's accounts t (@draw n_tr@)hat's a transfer: passengers lose it, the owner gains it, and f (@highlight rect effect box@)or society as a whole it nets to zero.

    pause 0.5

## The prize
domain: {"x":[0,240],"y":[0,20]}
vars:
    d:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 1
      step: 0.05
    cost:
      expr: 320 + 640*d
sources:
    - id: tullock67
      title: The Welfare Costs of Tariffs, Monopolies, and Theft
      authors: Tullock, G.
      year: 1967
      finding: 'The monopoly profit rectangle is not a costless transfer: resources spent to obtain (or defend) a monopoly, tariff or loot — lobbying, locks, thieves'' time — are a social cost on top of the Harberger triangle. Western Economic Journal 5(3): 224–232.'
    - id: krueger74
      title: The Political Economy of the Rent-Seeking Society
      authors: Krueger, A. O.
      year: 1974
      finding: 'Coins ''rent seeking'' for competition over government-created rents such as import licences. American Economic Review 64(3): 291–303.'
    - id: posner75
      title: The Social Costs of Monopoly and Regulation
      authors: Posner, R. A.
      year: 1975
      finding: 'If obtaining a monopoly is a competitive activity whose costs have no socially valuable by-products, the whole expected monopoly profit is dissipated. Journal of Political Economy 83(4): 807–827.'

    axes axes x_label "Tickets a day (Q)" y_label "Price ($)" cross corner ticks.x [80,160] ticks.y [4,12] hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "20 - 0.1*x" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_D "D" #b5482e at.data [204,1.2] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "4" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_MC "MC" #2f6b8f at.data [209,4] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pc #3d3833 at.on D at.x 160 hidden true
    text label_pc "Competitive: 160 at $4" at.data [196,5.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot pm #3d3833 at.on D at.x 80 hidden true
    text label_pm "Monopoly: 80 at $12" at.data [113,13.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    curve gh #8f887c expr "12" x_from 0 x_to 80 dash true hidden true
    curve gv #8f887c x_expr "80" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 12 dash true hidden true
    region dwl x_from 80 x_to 160 between ["D","MC"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text n_dwl "DWL $320" #b5482e at.data [165,8.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    region rect x_from 0 x_to 80 between ["gh","MC"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text n_prize "Prize $640" #8a6d1a at.data [40,9.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    curve bt #b5482e expr "4 + 8*d" x_from 0 x_to 80 hidden true
    region burn x_from 0 x_to 80 between ["bt","MC"] style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text readout "Cost to society: ${cost:0,} a day" #b5482e x 700 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    portrait tullock x 700 y 540 width 180 of "Gordon Tullock" hidden true
    icon bid1 size 70 set twemoji icon_look picture of ship in bidders hidden true
    icon bid2 size 70 set twemoji icon_look picture of "motor boat" in bidders hidden true
    icon bid3 size 70 set twemoji icon_look picture of speedboat in bidders hidden true
    row bidders gap 30 fit.h 80 fit.w 320 fit.x 540 fit.y 550 hidden true
    text n_bid "Three bidders" x 700 y 520 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon lock size 90 set twemoji x 700 y 560 icon_look picture of locked hidden true
    text n_rs "Rent seeking" #8a5fa8 x 700 y 580 font_size 44 hidden true
    text n_rs2 "Krueger, 1974" #8a5fa8 x 700 y 535 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch m work [{"tex":"Q_m = \\tfrac12 Q_c = 80"},{"tex":"\\text{Rect} = (P_m - MC)\\,Q_m"},{"tex":"\\text{Tri} = \\tfrac12 (P_m - MC)(Q_c - Q_m)"},{"tex":"\\text{Rect} = 2 \\times \\text{Tri}"}] x 775 y 565 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "The prize"

    draw axes D label_D MC label_MC pc label_pc pm label_pm gh gv dwl n_dwl

B: So the triangle is small. The r (@draw rect n_prize@)ectangle is not: six hundred and forty dollars a day, twice the triangle.

In 1967 Gor (@draw tullock@)don Tullock asked the obvious next question: how did the owner get that rectangle in the first place?

B: Say the harbour council hands out exactly o (@draw bid1 bid2 bid3 n_bid parallel true@)ne ferry licence, and three firms want it.
    erase tullock

Whoever wins co (@highlight rect n_prize effect box@)llects six hundred and forty dollars a day. So each firm hires lawyers, lobbies councillors and pays for studies proving it's the best.

B: Surely they spend only a little. Nobody bets the whole prize on a race they might lose.
    highlight bidders

    quiz "How much might the three firms spend, in all, fighting for the licence?" judge false
        right: About the whole prize, in Tullock's simplest case: as long as the expected prize beats the cost, another firm joins or bids more.
        * A little
        * About a third of the prize
        * About the whole prize

If anyo (@draw bt burn@)ne can join, firms keep raising their (@animate d 1 duration 2.5@) (@erase n_prize@) stakes until the expected prize is used up. The rectangle goes on lawyers' fees and lobbyists' hours.

B: The fees themselves only change hands. The waste is th (@highlight burn effect pulse@)e work: skilled hours that could have built boats, spent grabbing a prize instead.
    erase bid1 bid2 bid3 n_bid

So this monopoly costs not three hundred and tw (@draw readout@)enty dollars a day, but up to nine hundred and sixty: triangle plus rectangle, three times Harberger's count.

B: Tullock's paper made the same point about theft. The stolen money only moves; t (@draw lock@)he burglar's time and your new locks are pure waste.

    erase lock

Seven years later Anne Krueger gave the ide (@draw n_rs n_rs2@)a the name that stuck: rent seeking, spending real resources to win a transfer.

    erase n_rs n_rs2

B: Try it: slide how much of the prize gets burned, and watch the cost climb from the triangle to three times it.
    explore params ["d"]

    erase readout

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: Why is the rectangle exactly twice the triangle here?

@math

    erase mathq

With straight-line demand and a constant cost, a monopolist sells half the competitive quantity: eighty, not a hundred and sixty.
    draw m_box m_line_1

The r (@draw m_line_2@)ectangle is the markup tim (@draw m_line_3@)es eighty. The triangle is half the markup times the eighty lost trips.

Same markup, s (@draw m_line_4@)ame eighty, one with a half in front: the rectangle is always twice the triangle.

    erase m

@after

    erase mathq

    draw readout

    pause 0.4

## How much burns?
domain: {"x":[0,240],"y":[0,20]}
vars:
    d:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 1
      step: 0.05
    cost:
      expr: 320 + 640*d
sources:
    - id: opensecrets
      title: Federal lobbying spending, 2024
      authors: OpenSecrets (reported by the Washington Examiner)
      year: 2025
      finding: Registered federal lobbying in the US reached a record of about $4.4 billion in 2024.
      url: https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/politics/3320366/federal-lobbying-hit-record-4-4-billion-in-2024/
    - id: cbo
      title: The Federal Budget in Fiscal Year 2024
      authors: Congressional Budget Office
      year: 2025
      finding: Federal outlays in fiscal year 2024 were about $6.8 trillion.
    - id: ansolabehere
      title: Why Is There So Little Money in U.S. Politics?
      authors: Ansolabehere, S., de Figueiredo, J. M. & Snyder, J. M.
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Political spending is tiny relative to the value of government policy at stake — the puzzle often called the Tullock paradox. Journal of Economic Perspectives 17(1): 105–130.'
    - id: harberger
      title: Monopoly and Resource Allocation
      authors: Harberger, A. C.
      year: 1954
      finding: 'Welfare loss from monopoly in US manufacturing, 1924–28: roughly a tenth of one percent of national income.'
    - id: cm
      title: The Social Costs of Monopoly Power
      authors: Cowling, K. & Mueller, D. C.
      year: 1978
      finding: 'Counting monopoly profits (as rent-seeking costs) and advertising, welfare losses for 734 large US firms, 1963–66, of 4–13% of gross corporate product (UK: about 3–7%).'
      doi: 10.2307/2231975

    axes axes x_label "Tickets a day (Q)" y_label "Price ($)" cross corner ticks.x [80,160] ticks.y [4,12] hidden true
    curve D #b5482e expr "20 - 0.1*x" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_D "D" #b5482e at.data [204,1.2] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "4" x_from 0 x_to 200 hidden true
    text label_MC "MC" #2f6b8f at.data [209,4] font_size 26 hidden true
    dot pc #3d3833 at.on D at.x 160 hidden true
    text label_pc "Competitive: 160 at $4" at.data [196,5.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot pm #3d3833 at.on D at.x 80 hidden true
    text label_pm "Monopoly: 80 at $12" at.data [113,13.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    curve gh #8f887c expr "12" x_from 0 x_to 80 dash true hidden true
    curve gv #8f887c x_expr "80" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 12 dash true hidden true
    region dwl x_from 80 x_to 160 between ["D","MC"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text n_dwl "DWL $320" #b5482e at.data [165,8.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    region rect x_from 0 x_to 80 between ["gh","MC"] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    curve bt #b5482e expr "4 + 8*d" x_from 0 x_to 80 hidden true
    region burn x_from 0 x_to 80 between ["bt","MC"] style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text readout "Cost to society: ${cost:0,} a day" #b5482e x 700 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text lobby "US lobbying 2024: $4.4bn" cites opensecrets x 720 y 600 font_size 34 hidden true
    text paradox "Tullock paradox" #8a5fa8 x 720 y 545 font_size 38 hidden true
    text e1 "Harberger: 0.1%" cites harberger x 720 y 600 font_size 36 hidden true
    text e2 "Cowling–Mueller: 4–13%" cites cm x 720 y 545 font_size 36 hidden true
    text s1 "Triangle: consensus" #3d3833 x 720 y 600 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s2 "Full cost: contested" #b5482e x 720 y 545 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "How much burns?"

So the rectangle is a prize, and a  (@draw rect bt burn@)prize draws a fig (@animate d 1 duration 1.5@)ht. The open question is how much o (@draw readout@)f it really goes up in smoke.
    draw axes D label_D MC label_MC pc label_pc pm label_pm gh gv dwl n_dwl

B: Here's my objecti (@highlight burn effect box@)on. A full burn needs a race anyone can join. Many monopolies come from a pat (@animate d 0.5 duration 2.5 ghost true@)ent, a lucky head start or a friend on the council, with no open race at all.

And where there is a race, it often looks cheap. Registered lobbying in  (@draw lobby@)Washington cost about four point four billion dollars in 2024, next to a federal budget near seven trillion.

B: Tullock himself puzzled over why favours sell so ch (@draw paradox@)eaply. It's known as the Tullock paradox.

    erase lobby paradox

So the true cost lies somewhere between (@point at.data [40,8] gesture circle@) the triangle and the triangle plus the rectangle. Where depends on how open the fight is.

    erase bt_ghost burn_ghost

B: How big, then, across a whole economy? Harberger said about  (@draw e1@)a tenth of a percent of national income.

In 1978 Keith Cowling and Dennis Mueller counted Tullock's rectangles too, plus what large US firms spend on advertising to win market power. They go (@draw e2@)t four to thirteen percent of those firms' output.

    erase e1 e2

B: So that the triang (@draw s1@)le is real is consensus. How big th (@draw s2@)e full bill is, is still contested.

    pause 0.4

    erase s1 s2

    erase axes D label_D MC label_MC pc label_pc pm label_pm gh gv dwl n_dwl rect bt burn readout

    cards sortc size 2
        Waste: Lawyers' hours on a bid, Trips nobody takes, Burglar alarms
        Transfer: Higher fares still paid, Cash in a stolen wallet

    ask "Used up, or only moved? Sort each card." on sortc stage own store f voice b
        intro: Three questions on new cases.
        right: Right: lawyers' hours, lost trips and alarms use up something real; the higher fares and the stolen cash only change hands.
        wrong: You got {f} of {f.total}. Ask whether something is used up: lawyers' hours, lost trips and alarms are waste; fares and stolen cash only change hands.

    erase sortc

    draw axes D label_D MC label_MC pc label_pc pm label_pm gh gv dwl n_dwl rect bt burn readout

    quiz "A city has one licence for an airport bus. Which way of handing it out wastes the least?"
        right: The auction: the prize becomes city revenue, a transfer, instead of being burned in the race. The triangle stays either way.
        wrong: Ask where the prize ends up: as money someone can still spend, or as hours used up chasing it. Here, the auction.
        + Auction it; the money goes to the city
        * Give it to the firm that lobbies hardest
        * Let firms compete with ads and studies for a year

    quiz "A new monopoly earns $10 million a year in profit, and its deadweight triangle is $5 million. Rivals can freely compete to own it. Up to how much could it cost society each year?" voice b
        right: $15 million: the $5 million triangle, plus up to the whole $10 million prize burned in the race to win it.
        wrong: Count what vanishes, and what gets fought over: here, up to $15 million.
        * $5 million
        * $10 million
        + $15 million

So how much does monopoly cost society? The trips  (@highlight dwl burn effect pulse@)that never happen, plus whatever is burned in the fight to own the toll booth, and the second can be the bigger bill.

    pause 0.5

